SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}After curative surgery for colorectal cancer, each 5\,$\mu$g\,m$^{-3}$ of 1-year-lagged residential PM$_{2.5}$ carried a hazard ratio of 1.079 (95\,\% CI 1.045--1.115) for death in 7068 Chongqing patients, and among PM$_{2.5}$ constituents organic matter was the one that held up (HR per IQR 1.088, 1.028--1.152).}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
Chongqing Medical University, \textit{Journal of Hazardous Materials}. Four hospitals, median follow-up 56 months, 1540 deaths; time-varying Cox models on CHAP pollutant surfaces and TAP constituents; PM$_{10}$ HR 1.050 (1.027--1.074) per 5\,$\mu$g\,m$^{-3}$; associations stable in two-pollutant models, and OM survived residual and remaining-mass models.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} the outcome is all-cause, not cancer-specific, death; exposure is fixed to the address at diagnosis; tumour stage and treatment quality can be patterned by hospital catchment and therefore by pollution; constituent surfaces are model products whose errors are correlated with total mass.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} post-diagnosis exposure is a modifiable window for a defined patient group, and the constituent that carries the signal --- organic matter --- is exactly the fraction an optical mass sensor cannot separate. Survivorship cohorts are small enough for home-level sensing, if paired with a composition proxy."""

